# Valgrind suppressions for the test suite.
#
# Same rule as tests/tsan.supp: every entry carries the argument for why it is
# not a bug. A suppression file without those turns the tool into a machine for
# producing green ticks.

# The crypto backends and libjuice read uninitialised bytes deliberately, as
# entropy or as part of constant-time code that touches both branches. The
# reads are of memory they own and the values never leave the library.
{
   crypto-and-ice-libraries-read-their-own-uninitialised-bytes
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:*/libcrypto.so*
}
{
   crypto-and-ice-libraries-read-their-own-uninitialised-bytes-value
   Memcheck:Value8
   obj:*/libcrypto.so*
}

# libssh asks the crypto backend for random bytes while it negotiates a key
# exchange, and the backend answers out of a per-thread generator that only
# its own thread-exit hook releases. The tests reach it twice over: the block
# is charged to a forkpty() child that inherited it, and that child is
# signalled dead inside login_tty() before it can exec, so no hook of any kind
# runs. Neither process allocated it and neither could free it.
{
   crypto-per-thread-generator-outlives-the-thread-that-asked-for-random
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   ...
   fun:CRYPTO_THREAD_set_local_ex
   ...
}

# mount() ignores its filesystemtype argument for a bind, a remount and a
# propagation change, and NULL is the idiomatic value there -- util-linux
# passes it too. Valgrind 3.22, which is what Ubuntu 24.04 and so the CI
# runners carry, checks the argument unconditionally and calls the NULL
# unaddressable; newer valgrind does not report it. Every mount() in comrade
# that passes no type is one of those three operations, so there is no call
# here for which the kernel would read the pointer.
#
# It is not a cosmetic complaint: MEMORYCHECK_COMMAND_OPTIONS carries
# --error-exitcode=1, so the finding made the confined child exit 1, the test
# read that as its RC_FAIL, and sandbox_test aborted on the assertion --
# a false positive presenting as a failed confinement.
{
   mount-ignores-its-type-for-bind-remount-and-propagation
   Memcheck:Param
   mount(type)
   fun:mount
   ...
}
